# Published open data, not an image input: the Dockerfile copies named paths and never this one,
# so keeping it out of the build context spares the daemon a transfer nothing in the image uses.
exports

# Environment files never enter the build context, so no layer — and no layer cache — can carry a
# secret even if a Dockerfile line were added that copied one. This is the second half of the rule
# the Dockerfile states: secrets reach the container through the ECS task definition's `secrets:`
# array, never through the image. `scripts/check-deploy.mjs` fails CI if either half is removed.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# The dashboard is not an input to the API image. It is deployed on its own (see its README) and
# the API image must not be able to fail — and so block an ECS deploy — on a package the service
# never runs, which `pnpm -r build` / `pnpm -r typecheck` in the builder stage would otherwise do.
#
# NOTE for whoever adds packages/dashboard: the builder runs `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`, and
# a workspace member present in pnpm-lock.yaml but missing from the context makes that command
# refuse the lockfile. Excluding the directory therefore has to come with keeping the dashboard out
# of the API's lockfile importers (its own lockfile / not a `packages/*` workspace member), or with
# copying it in and building only the API package. Validate with a local `docker build` at that point.
packages/dashboard
